WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.844
Account A-1001 wants its 20 newest payments.

2
00:00:05.844 --> 00:00:08.559
The payments company keeps 50 million.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:14.402
They live in Cassandra, a free database program that spreads its data over many computers.

4
00:00:14.402 --> 00:00:20.041
Each computer is called a node, and together they work as one database, called a cluster.

5
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.622
So how does it hand back 20 rows without touching the other 50 million?

6
00:00:24.622 --> 00:00:27.424
And why does it flatly refuse some questions?

7
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.857
Picture an office with a drawer of payment slips for each account, filed by date, newest at the front.

8
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:38.957
To find A-1001's newest slips, you open one drawer and lift the front few out.

9
00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:40.724
The other drawers stay shut.

10
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.046
Cassandra keeps rows the same way.

11
00:00:43.046 --> 00:00:45.130
The drawer is called a partition.

12
00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:50.307
Every row with the same account goes into it, so the account is the partition key.

13
00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:58.644
The order inside comes from the clustering columns: the time it was created, newest first, then the payment id, to break ties.

14
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.668
But which node holds the drawer?

15
00:01:00.668 --> 00:01:05.512
The node that takes your request, called the coordinator, hashes the partition key.

16
00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:11.903
It turns A-1001 into a big whole number called a token, always the same for the same key.

17
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:17.864
Picture every possible token around a circle, with each node in charge of some stretches of it.

18
00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:24.359
The token's place picks the nodes that keep a copy of the drawer, usually three, called replicas.

19
00:01:24.359 --> 00:01:25.663
Only they get asked.

20
00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:31.600
Where the office picture breaks: paper has one copy, and each replica holds the whole drawer.

21
00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:33.179
Now open the drawer.

22
00:01:33.179 --> 00:01:35.204
Four payments, newest first.

23
00:01:35.204 --> 00:01:38.964
The 20 newest are the first 20 rows; here, all four.

24
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.132
A new payment arrives, timed nine o'clock, and written last.

25
00:01:43.132 --> 00:01:44.274
Where does it go?

26
00:01:44.274 --> 00:01:45.349
Pause and guess.

27
00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:50.845
Between nine forty and eight fifteen.

28
00:01:50.845 --> 00:01:52.570
Only the account was hashed.

29
00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:57.312
The time says where inside the drawer it goes, and no other row is rewritten.

30
00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:02.839
If you guessed the bottom, that's the spreadsheet habit: add at the end, sort when you read.

31
00:02:02.839 --> 00:02:05.992
Here the order is how rows sit, in memory and on disk.

32
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:08.910
So ask for this morning, from eight to ten.

33
00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:17.112
The node jumps to the first row before ten, reads on while rows are inside the window, and stops at the first row before eight.

34
00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:19.335
Three rows read, three returned.

35
00:02:19.335 --> 00:02:23.019
The ten thirty row and yesterday's row are never touched.

36
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:24.202
Oldest first?

37
00:02:24.202 --> 00:02:27.469
Same three rows, read from the other end.

38
00:02:27.469 --> 00:02:28.584
Still no sort.

39
00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:30.619
Now the question it refuses.

40
00:02:30.619 --> 00:02:33.852
Every failed payment, without naming an account.

41
00:02:33.852 --> 00:02:36.007
No account, no token, no drawer.

42
00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:39.923
The only plan is to open every drawer on every node.

43
00:02:39.923 --> 00:02:46.920
So CQL, the language you query Cassandra in, refuses, unless you add two words: ALLOW FILTERING.

44
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:49.447
That's the database asking: are you sure?

45
00:02:49.447 --> 00:02:52.349
You're about to open every drawer in the building.

46
00:02:52.533 --> 00:02:54.841
So the rules follow from the picture.

47
00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:58.271
The partition key needs an equals, or there's no token.

48
00:02:58.271 --> 00:03:01.577
Clustering columns can be limited only from the left.

49
00:03:01.577 --> 00:03:04.571
And a different question gets a different table.

50
00:03:04.733 --> 00:03:05.926
One more catch.

51
00:03:05.926 --> 00:03:14.112
A drawer never splits across nodes, and the Cassandra docs advise under 100,000 rows and 100 megabytes.

52
00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:18.412
Its size is the write rate times how long the key stays the same.

53
00:03:18.412 --> 00:03:22.621
Key a shop's payments by shop alone, and the drawer grows forever.

54
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.557
Add the day: 40,000 payments a day, about 6 megabytes.

55
00:03:27.557 --> 00:03:27.997
Fine.

56
00:03:28.167 --> 00:03:34.961
But on the busiest day of the year, the biggest shop takes 5 a second: 432,000 rows.

57
00:03:34.961 --> 00:03:37.631
One drawer per hour gives 18,000.

58
00:03:37.767 --> 00:03:43.650
The price: a week of history is now 168 drawers, read in parallel and merged.

59
00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.865
Size it for your busiest key.

60
00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:52.291
This shape gives the family its name, wide-column, because one partition can hold a great many rows.

61
00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:57.164
It has nothing to do with a column store, which keeps each column's values together.

62
00:03:57.164 --> 00:03:59.529
Deep drawer would have been a better name.

63
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.332
Here's the key, read from the inside out.

64
00:04:02.332 --> 00:04:04.963
Inside the double brackets, which drawer.

65
00:04:04.963 --> 00:04:06.696
After it, the order inside.

66
00:04:06.867 --> 00:04:15.449
So the partition key picks the drawer, the clustering columns pick the place in it, and a good question opens one drawer and lifts out one run.

67
00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:17.053
One quick check.

68
00:04:17.053 --> 00:04:22.255
A query names A-1001 and payment pay-81723, but no time.

69
00:04:22.255 --> 00:04:24.112
One run, or refused?

70
00:04:24.112 --> 00:04:25.599
Pause and think.

71
00:04:28.833 --> 00:04:29.464
Refused.

72
00:04:29.464 --> 00:04:34.511
The rows are in time order, so an id with no time isn't one run.

73
00:04:34.511 --> 00:04:37.429
Without ALLOW FILTERING, CQL says no.
